-- title:
--   Pinned specialisation family for the norm-free part at p ‖ M
-- statement:
--   Fix a natural number $M\neq 0$ and a prime $p$, and assume $p \mid M$, $p^2 \nmid M$ and $5 \le M/p$. Let $P$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` satisfying `P.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ lies in the non-units of $P$. Write $G = \overline{\mathbb{Q}} \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[\mathbb{Q}]} \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the absolute Galois group, $I_P =$ `P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` for the image in $G$ of the inertia subgroup of $P$ under the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup, $\kappa =$ `IsLocalRing.ResidueField P` with residue map `IsLocalRing.residue P`, and $\bar F =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC κ M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) for the $q$-expansion function field of $X_1(M)$ over $\kappa$ (the subfield of `LaurentSeries κ` generated over $\kappa$ by the integral form ratios for $\Gamma_1(M)$). Write $J_1(M) =$ [`ModularCurve.JOne M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the group `Pic0` of the base-changed function field `laurentBaseChange (AlgebraicClosure ℚ) (x1FunctionField M)`, i.e. degree-zero divisors on that function field modulo principal divisors, with its $G$-action.
--
--   Call a subgroup $I \le G$ *admissible* when it comes with the three data appearing in the statement: $I \le I_P$; every $\sigma \in I$ fixes every $\zeta \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $\zeta^p = 1$; and `I.subgroupOf (P.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ)` has finite index, i.e. $I \cap I_P$ has finite index in $I_P$.
--
--   The assertion is the existence of a family $\mathcal{D}$ which assigns to every admissible $I$ (together with its three proofs) a term
--   $$\mathcal{D}_I : \mathtt{QExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3}\ (\mathtt{x1FunctionField } M)\ P\ I\ p\ \kappa\ (\mathtt{residue } P)\ \bar F\ (\mathtt{x1FunctionField }(M/p))\ (\mathtt{x1FunctionFieldC } \kappa\ (M/p)),$$
--   subject to the twelve further clauses below. The structure `QExpSemistableSpecializationPinnedV3` packages, for the given level-$M$ data: a finite set `nodes` of pairs of places of $\bar F$ over $\kappa$, each place in a pair having residue field generated by $\kappa$; a semilinear automorphism `frob` of $\bar F$ over $\kappa$ raising every Laurent coefficient to the $p$-th power; a subgroup `dom` of $J_1(M)$ all of whose elements are fixed by $I$ and which is stable under any $\varphi$ with `P.IsFrobeniusAt φ p` normalising $I$; a homomorphism `sp` from `dom` to `GluedPic0 κ F̄ nodes` (admissible gluing data modulo glued principal data), injective on prime-to-$p$ torsion, surjective onto prime-to-$p$ torsion, together with the resulting finiteness and cardinality identity for the $n$-torsion of `Pic0` for $n$ prime to $p$, and a Frobenius compatibility for the first component; the last two parameters supply the level-$M/p$ function field over $\mathbb{Q}$ and over $\kappa$, which enter the remaining pinning requirements of the structure. Throughout the clauses, $\nu_I(y) :=$ [`AlgebraicCurve.GluedPic0.toPic0Pair _ ((𝒟 I …).sp ⟨y, hy⟩)`](def/AlgebraicCurve_GluedPic0.html#L229) denotes the pair of `Pic0 κ F̄`-classes obtained from `sp` by passing from a glued class to the pair of classes of its two divisor components; $A' =$ [`ModularCurve.normFreePartAt M p`](def/ModularCurve_X1PrimitiveSpecializationAtP.html#L29) denotes the image of the endomorphism $y \mapsto \#S \cdot y - \sum_{d \in S} \langle d\rangle y$ of $J_1(M)$, where $S =$ `normFreeRepsAt M p` is the set of $d < M$ with $\gcd(d,M)=1$ and $d \equiv 1 \pmod{M/p}$ and $\langle d \rangle =$ `diamondOneBar M d` is the diamond endomorphism induced by the base-changed diamond automorphism. In clauses (in∞) onwards the two admissible subgroups $I$, $I'$ are quantified independently, with no inclusion between them required.
--
--   (nest) For admissible $I$ and $I'$ with $I' \le I$ and every $y \in J_1(M)$ lying in `dom` of $\mathcal{D}_I$, one has $y \in$ `dom` of $\mathcal{D}_{I'}$ and $\nu_{I'}(y) = \nu_I(y)$.
--
--   (dom) For admissible $I$, every $y \in A'$ fixed by all $\sigma \in I$ lies in `dom` of $\mathcal{D}_I$.
--
--   (in∞) For $\sigma \in I_P$, admissible $I, I'$, and $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\sigma \cdot y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$: the first components satisfy $\nu_{I'}(\sigma \cdot y)_1 = \nu_I(y)_1$.
--
--   (in0) For $\sigma \in I_P$ which fixes every $p$-th root of unity, admissible $I, I'$, and $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\sigma \cdot y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$: the second components satisfy $\nu_{I'}(\sigma \cdot y)_2 = \nu_I(y)_2$.
--
--   (diam0) For $\sigma \in I_P$ and $u \in \mathbb{N}$ with $u \equiv 1 \pmod{M/p}$ such that $\sigma \zeta = \zeta^u$ for every $\zeta$ with $\zeta^p = 1$, admissible $I, I'$, and $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\langle u \rangle(\sigma \cdot y) \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$: $\nu_{I'}(\langle u \rangle(\sigma \cdot y))_2 = \nu_I(y)_2$.
--
--   (diam∞) For every $u \equiv 1 \pmod{M/p}$ there is an additive endomorphism $\theta$ of `Pic0 κ F̄` such that for all admissible $I, I'$ and all $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\langle u \rangle y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$ one has $\nu_{I'}(\langle u \rangle y)_1 = \theta(\nu_I(y)_1)$.
--
--   (dec0) For every $\sigma$ in the decomposition subgroup `P.decompositionSubgroup ℚ` there is an additive endomorphism $\theta$ of `Pic0 κ F̄` such that for all admissible $I, I'$ and all $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\sigma \cdot y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$ one has $\nu_{I'}(\sigma \cdot y)_2 = \theta(\nu_I(y)_2)$.
--
--   (frob∞) For every $\varphi \in G$ with `P.IsFrobeniusAt φ p` (that is, $\varphi$ lies in the decomposition subgroup and acts on $\kappa$ as $x \mapsto x^p$), all admissible $I, I'$, and all $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $\varphi \cdot y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$: $\nu_{I'}(\varphi \cdot y)_1 = \mathcal{D}_I.\mathtt{frob} \cdot \nu_I(y)_1$.
--
--   (U∞) For all admissible $I, I'$ and all $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $U_p y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$, where $U_p =$ `heckeOperatorOneBar M ⟨p, _⟩` is the Hecke operator at $p$ on $J_1(M)$: if $\nu_I(y)_2 = 0$ then $\nu_{I'}(U_p y)_1 = p \cdot (\mathcal{D}_I.\mathtt{frob}^{-1} \cdot \nu_I(y)_1)$, the integer $p$ acting by scalar multiplication.
--
--   (U0) In the same situation, if $\nu_I(y)_2 = 0$ then $\nu_{I'}(U_p y)_2 = 0$.
--
--   (U0Φ) There is an additive automorphism $\Phi$ of `Pic0 κ F̄` such that for all admissible $I, I'$ and all $y \in A'$ with $y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ and $U_p y \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_{I'})$ one has $\nu_{I'}(U_p y)_2 = \Phi(\nu_I(y)_2)$.
--
--   (conn) Let $x \in$ [`TateModule p (JOne M)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), i.e. a sequence $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ in $J_1(M)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$, and assume that every $x_n$ lies in $A'$, and that for every $n$, every admissible $I$ and every proof that $x_n \in$ `dom`$(\mathcal{D}_I)$ one has $\nu_I(x_n) = 0$. Then every $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear functional $f$ on [`TateModule p (JOne M)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) which satisfies $f(\tau \cdot z) = f(z)$ — the action being [`TateModule.rep`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L174), coordinatewise — for all $\tau \in I_P$ fixing every $p$-th root of unity and all $z$ in the Tate module with all coordinates in $A'$, vanishes at $x$: $f(x) = 0$.
--
--   This is the geometric input for the local analysis of $J_1(M)$ at a prime $p$ exactly dividing $M$: it produces, uniformly in the admissible inertia subgroups $I$ at a place $P$ above $p$, semistable $q$-expansion specialisation data for $X_1(M)$ together with the compatibilities of the two-component reduction with inertia, the decomposition group, the diamond operators $\langle u \rangle$ for $u \equiv 1 \pmod{M/p}$, Frobenius and the Hecke operator $U_p$, in the Deligne–Rapoport/Katz–Mazur picture of the special fibre as two Igusa components. It is used to derive the relations satisfied by the Galois action on the $p$-adic Tate module of $J_1(M)$ on the norm-free part, namely the statements [`ModularCurve.rep_diamondGen_apply_inertia_sub_eq_of_nsmul_sub_sum_tateModule_jOne_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.rep_diamondGen_apply_inertia_sub_eq_of_nsmul_sub_sum_tateModule_jOne_of_dvd_of_not_sq_dvd_of_le_div) and [`ModularCurve.rep_frobenius_rep_heckeGenOne_sub_smul_rep_diamondGen_rep_inertia_sub_eq_zero_normFreePartAt_tateModule_jOne_of_le_div`](thm.html#ModularCurve.rep_frobenius_rep_heckeGenOne_sub_smul_rep_diamondGen_rep_inertia_sub_eq_zero_normFreePartAt_tateModule_jOne_of_le_div).
